package app.pub.builder

import app.pub.core.FilesContract
import app.pub.core.PhoneFilesContract

/**
 * Exact file vocabulary supplied to the model: the app's private folder (storage.files) and the files the user picks
 * on the phone (hardware capability files). Scripts never receive phone paths, content URIs or provider names.
 */
object FileGuide {
    val CONTRACT = """
        Private files (capability storage.files) are optional. Each app has its own private folder on this phone for documents, data files, logs, collections and organizing its photos. Only this app sees it: not other PUB apps, other Android apps, the Files app, shared storage or Downloads. Nothing is uploaded; it is deleted with the app. Declare storage.files only if the app uses files; storage.project stays the place for small settings and state.
        Emit {type:'files.request',id:'short_unique_id',operation:'op',args:{...}}; always include args ({} when there are none). id uses letters/digits/_/- (max 64), unique among your pending requests. Requests count toward the 4 effects per dispatch, need no button or permission, and may be sent from init.
        Results arrive asynchronously as a later event matched by id, in request order: {type:'files',id,operation,ok:true,...result} or {type:'files',id,operation,ok:false,code,message}. The dispatch that sends a request must already return a complete UI (a loading line or the empty state), then update it when the reply arrives. At most ${FilesContract.MAX_PENDING} requests may wait for replies; more are answered with code busy.
        Error codes: not_found, exists, not_empty, is_directory, not_directory, invalid_path, invalid_arguments, quota_exceeded, read_only, unavailable, busy. Show a short helpful message; never retry in a loop.
        Paths are relative to the app's folder, '/'-separated, case-sensitive, with no leading or trailing '/'; '' or '.' is the folder itself. Each name is 1-${FilesContract.MAX_NAME_CHARS} ASCII letters, digits, spaces, '.', '_', '-', '(' or ')' and cannot start with '.'; at most ${FilesContract.MAX_DEPTH} levels and ${FilesContract.MAX_PATH_CHARS} characters. Keep user-facing titles in an index file and generate safe names such as 'note-17.txt'.
        Operations (args -> ok result fields):
        list {path?:'',recursive?:false,offset?:0} -> entries:[{path,name,type:'file'|'directory',size,modified}],total,truncated,nextOffset. Sorted by path; one reply holds at most ${FilesContract.MAX_LIST_ENTRIES} entries (fewer with long names): when truncated, list again with offset:nextOffset. size is bytes (0 for folders); modified is milliseconds since 1970.
        stat {path} -> entry (same shape) or code not_found.
        read {path,offset?:0,length?:${FilesContract.MAX_CHUNK_BYTES},encoding:'utf8'|'base64'} -> data,offset,bytesRead,size,eof. One reply holds at most ${FilesContract.MAX_CHUNK_BYTES} bytes; utf8 replies may hold fewer because they contain only whole characters. Continue at offset+bytesRead until eof, joining the data; parse JSON only after eof. Bytes that are not UTF-8 text need base64.
        write {path,data,encoding:'utf8'|'base64',append?:false,createParents?:true} -> size. data is at most ${FilesContract.MAX_CHUNK_BYTES} bytes after decoding, and the whole packet still has to fit 32768 characters: send text in pieces of at most 8000 characters (base64: 12000 bytes = 16000 characters). Without append the file is replaced atomically: a reader never sees a half-written file. With append the data is added at the end. For larger content write the first piece without append, then each next piece with append:true when the previous reply arrives.
        ${FilesContract.MAX_CHUNK_BYTES} bytes is only what one request carries, never a limit on a file: a file can grow to the folder's quota by appending pieces and is read back in pieces. Do not cap what users can store (an entry, note or document) at one request's size; take any size limit from the use case and the quota, and show progress while a large file is written or read.
        mkdir {path} creates the folder and missing parents -> created (false if it already existed).
        delete {path,recursive?:false} -> deleted (entries removed). A folder that is not empty needs recursive:true (otherwise not_empty).
        move {from,to,overwrite?:false} renames or moves a file or folder; copy {from,to,overwrite?:false} copies one -> entry of the new path. Missing parent folders of to are created. An existing to fails with exists; overwrite:true replaces an existing file with a file. Folders are never overwritten.
        usage {} -> bytes, files (files and folders), quotaBytes, quotaFiles, photos, photoBytes.
        Quota per app: ${FilesContract.QUOTA_BYTES / (1024 * 1024)} MiB and ${FilesContract.QUOTA_ENTRIES} files and folders, apart from photos. A change that would exceed it fails with quota_exceeded before anything is written.
        photos: the folder always contains a read-only folder named photos that lists this app's camera photos as photos/<asset>, the same 'UUID.jpg' asset as in the camera photo event and the image node. List {path:'photos'} at init to find every photo the app has taken, also in later sessions, without storing each asset: show one with {type:'image',asset:entry.name,description:'...'} and keep titles or notes per asset in your index file. delete {path:'photos/<asset>'} deletes that photo. copy from photos is allowed (the copy counts toward the files quota). write, mkdir and move into or out of photos fail with read_only. Photos are taken only with the camera capability (hardware guide).
        Good practice: keep an index file (for example index.json with titles, dates and file names) and rewrite it after each change; on first run its read fails with not_found, which means empty. Operations that take several requests (chunked reads and writes, copying a list of files) must show progress such as 'Saving 2 of 5' and disable conflicting buttons until they finish.
        Previews: an unsaved preview's files are temporary until Save locally keeps them. An edit preview starts with a copy of the saved app's files; its changes are discarded when it closes or on Save changes, which keeps the saved app's files, and the saved app's photos are read-only there.
        Behavior tests and the visual review answer files requests from a simulated private folder that starts empty (photos is empty too) and otherwise works like the real one, separately for each test. The visual review shows the screen after the replies to init's requests, so it sees the app's empty state. A test may save something and then expect to see it (for example tap Save, then expect the new title in the list); it can never find files from earlier sessions. Every dispatch must still return a complete UI before its replies arrive.

        Files on the phone (hardware capability files) are optional and separate from the private folder: documents and folders the user picks in Android's system file picker, including Downloads, shared storage and apps that offer documents such as Drive. The app sees only what the user picks, by opaque handle, name, size and type, never paths or locations; it cannot browse the phone or read other apps' private data. Declare files only if the app opens or saves files the user chooses; import and export also need storage.files.
        Requests are hardware requests {type:'hardware.request',id,capability:'files',operation,args}; replies are {type:'hardware',id,capability:'files',event,data}; failures are event 'error' with data {code,message}.
        pick opens the picker and must come from a button action, never from init or a reply: pick {mode:'open'|'openMultiple'|'folder'|'create', mimeTypes?:['text/*','application/pdf'] (open/openMultiple, 1-${PhoneFilesContract.MAX_MIME_TYPES}, default all), write?:false (open/openMultiple/folder; true only if the app will change what is picked), max?:1-${PhoneFilesContract.MAX_PICK} (openMultiple), name (create: suggested file name), mimeType? (create)}. Event picked {items:[{handle,name,kind:'file'|'folder',mimeType,size?,canRead,canWrite}],truncated} or cancelled {}. create makes a new empty document where the user chooses ('Save as') and returns its handle for writing. The picker covers PUB, so {type:'hardwareStopped'} may arrive while it is open; the picked or cancelled reply still follows.
        The first files request of a session may show PUB's permission dialog, so start with a button. Once the user allowed access (or always allows it for this app), the other operations also work from init and from replies.
        grants {} -> grants {items,max:${PhoneFilesContract.MAX_GRANTS}}: every file and folder this app still holds. Call it at init to reopen documents from earlier sessions; keep your own labels keyed by handle in storage.project or the private index file. release {handle} -> released {handle} ends access; the document stays.
        On a file handle: stat {handle} -> entry {entry:{name,kind,mimeType,size?,modified?,canWrite}}; read {handle,offset?,length?:1-${FilesContract.MAX_CHUNK_BYTES},encoding:'utf8'|'base64'} -> data {data,offset,bytesRead,size?,eof}, chunked like private files (continue at offset+bytesRead until eof); write {handle,data,encoding,append?} -> written {size?} replaces the contents (append adds to the end; some storage apps cannot append: unavailable); delete {handle} -> deleted {handle,released:true} deletes the document itself.
        On a folder handle, path is relative to the picked folder ('' is the folder itself). Names are the documents' real names in any language, without '/', up to ${PhoneFilesContract.MAX_DEPTH} levels. list {handle,path?,offset?} -> entries {entries:[{name,path?,kind,mimeType,size?,modified?,canWrite}],total,truncated,nextOffset?,incomplete?}, sorted by name; continue with offset:nextOffset; an entry without path cannot be addressed. stat and read take {handle,path,...}; write {handle,path,data,encoding,append?} creates the file and missing folders -> written {size?,created,entry}; mkdir {handle,path} -> created {created,entry}; delete {handle,path,recursive?} -> deleted {path} (a folder that is not empty needs recursive:true); rename {handle,path,name} -> renamed {entry}. The picked folder itself cannot be deleted or renamed. A storage app that names a new file differently returns the real name in entry.
        delete always needs a button the user just tapped, after asking them to confirm; it deletes the user's document.
        import {handle,path?,to,overwrite?} copies a picked file (or a file in a picked folder) into the private folder at to -> imported {entry}. export {from,handle,path?,overwrite?} copies a private file over a picked writable file, or to path in a picked folder -> exported {bytes,entry?}. export {from,createName,mimeType?} opens the 'Save as' picker for a new document (button only) -> exported {bytes,item} or cancelled. Both stream whole files within the private folder's quota; prefer them to chunked read and write for anything large.
        Error codes: gesture_required (start from a button), denied or blocked (the user or Settings refused; explain how to allow it in Settings → Access), busy (a picker is open or ${PhoneFilesContract.MAX_PENDING} requests wait), revoked (access was removed outside the app: ask the user to pick again), not_found (handle, file or folder gone: offer to pick again), read_only (picked without write, the saved app's files in an edit preview, or the storage app forbids it), grant_limit (release some first), exists, not_empty, is_directory, not_directory, invalid_path, invalid_arguments, quota_exceeded, unavailable (the storage app does not support it). Show a short message; never retry in a loop.
        Good patterns: buttons such as Open file, Open folder, Import, Export and Save as…; show the picked name; restore handles with grants at init and treat not_found and revoked as 'pick again'; confirm before deleting; show progress for chunked work.
        Saved apps keep access to what was picked until the app releases it or the user forgets it in Settings → Access; deleting the app never deletes the documents. In a preview, what is picked lasts until the preview closes and is not kept by Save locally (the saved app asks again). An edit preview also sees the saved app's picked files through grants, read-only. Behavior tests and the visual review never open the picker: pick and export to a new document answer cancelled, grants answers no items, and other requests answer not_found.
    """.trimIndent()
}
